    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d296">

                <pb facs="0674" n="646" sameAs="frus:frus1955-57v02/pg646"/>

                <head>296. Memorandum From the Secretary of State to the President<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d296-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1955-07-12"><hi rendition="#italic">July 12,
                        1955</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>I have sent you my cable to Macmillan with reference to a communication to Chou
                        En-lai,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d296-f2"/> designed to carry out that portion
                    of your letter to Prime Minister Nehru<anchor n="3" xml:id="d296-f3"/> which
                    speaks of designating an ambassador to carry on the talks at Geneva with the
                    Chinese Communist régime.</p>
                <p>I gather that both Nehru and Macmillan have misinterpreted this statement as
                    indicating that the Chinese Communists know that we were not prepared to discuss
                    other topics unless and until the question of exchange of citizens was
                    satisfactorily worked out. That had never been my intent, nor I think yours, and
                    I think the text of the suggested message to Chou En-lai contained in my cable
                    to Macmillan makes this clear.</p>
                <p>I am suggesting that you might want to make a reply to Nehru along the lines of
                    the attached.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d296-f4"/></p>
                <pb facs="0675" n="647" xml:id="pg647"/>
                <p>I have just heard from Macmillan<anchor n="5" xml:id="d296-f5"/> that he is in
                    complete agreement with the text of the proposed oral communication to be
                    delivered to Chou En-lai.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">JFD</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
